INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','barnet','barnet-1471','Battle of Barnet',1471,0.28,'Easter Sunday, 14 April 1471 (Julian calendar; 23 April by the modern calendar). Secure. Edward reached Barnet on the evening of 13 April and deployed in the dark; the Arrivall says the fight began between four and five in the morning and lasted about three hours, Warkworth from four until ten.',51.6655,-0.199,1,NULL,'The point given is the road fork at Hadley Highstone, between the registered area (Hadley Green) and the Kitts End alternative. Treat it as the middle of a zone of possibility, not a site.','{"position": [50, 330.0, 820], "target": [35, 68.0, -330]}','Edward IV, back from exile, marched out of London on 13 April 1471 and in the dark drew up his army close to that of the Earl of Warwick, which was already in position north of Barnet across the road to St Albans. They fought at dawn on Easter Sunday in thick mist, with the lines misaligned so that each overlapped the other at one end. The king''s left was broken and fled through the town, but in the confusion Warwick''s army collapsed; Warwick and his brother Montagu were killed. Where exactly on this ridge it happened is not known.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/barnet/episodes.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','brentford','brentford-1016','Battle of Brentford',1016,0.5,'1016; day and month not recorded. In the Chronicle it comes ''within two nights'' of Edmund''s relief of London, after Cnut''s siege had begun in May and after Edmund''s fights at Penselwood and Sherston, and before the battle of Assandun and Edmund''s death on St Andrew''s day (30 November). Summer is likely but is an inference.',51.4803,-0.3022,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [-730, 380, 301], "target": [74, 0, 297]}','In 1016 Cnut''s army besieged London. Edmund Ironside gathered a force, came to the city along the north side of the Thames and drove the besiegers to their ships. Within two nights he crossed the Thames at Brentford, fought the Danish army and put it to flight; many of the English were drowned, which the Chronicle blames on their own carelessness in going ahead of the army for plunder. Edmund then went to Wessex for more men and the Danes returned to London. That is all that is recorded. The scene shows the river, the ford and the banks as best they can be placed, with plain blocks of figures and shaded areas for where the armies may have been. Every one of those is a guess, and the model does not show the fight itself.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/brentford/episodes.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/boudica-60','london','boudica-60','Boudica''s sack of Londinium',60,0.55,'AD 60 or 61; season unknown. Tacitus puts the revolt under the consuls Caesennius Paetus and Petronius Turpilianus (the consuls of AD 61), but many scholars prefer 60 on the grounds that the events need more than one year. The RCHME volume on Roman London prefers 60; MOLA writes ''AD 60/61''. A Bloomberg writing tablet dated 21 October AD 62 shows London trading again by then, which fits either year. Use 60 with a visible ''or 61''.',51.5115,-0.0865,1,NULL,'Site well known in general, not in detail. The pre-revolt town lay on the eastern hill (Cornhill) between the Walbrook and the area north of the bridgehead, with some occupation west of the Walbrook and a suburb at the south end of the bridge in Southwark. The burnt horizon has been recorded on many City sites (King William Street, Lombard Street, Eastcheap, Miles Lane, Fenchurch Street, the Walbrook west bank) and beside the road at the Southwark bridgehead. The point given is an approximate centre of the early town, not a find spot.','{"position": [-480, 360, 620], "target": [60, 10, -250]}','During the revolt led by Boudica of the Iceni, the governor Suetonius Paulinus reached Londinium ahead of the rebels, judged he had too few troops to hold it, and withdrew, taking those who could march with him. The rebels then destroyed the town and killed those who stayed. Excavation across the City and at the Southwark bridgehead has found a layer of burnt debris of this date. The town was rebuilt within a few years and a fort was built on the eastern hill about AD 63.','attested-excavated','high','locations/london/episodes.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','london','london-bridge-1014','Battle of London Bridge',1014,0.5,'Uncertain. Snorri places it after Swein Forkbeard''s death (February 1014) as part of Aethelred''s return, hence the usual ''1014''; Hagland and Watson accept 1014 following Ian Howard''s chronology. Olaf''s early career is poorly dated and he is also associated with Thorkell''s army, which attacked London unsuccessfully in 1009-10, so some place his London fighting then, and on the Danish side. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle for 1014 says only that Aethelred came home in Lent and was gladly received.',51.5079,-0.0868,1,NULL,'The place is known; the event is not. A timber bridge existed by about 1000 (first phase built of timber felled c. 987-1032) on roughly the line of the later medieval bridge, a little downstream of the present London Bridge, with Southwark at its south end. The point given is approximate mid-river on that line.','{"position": [330, 70, 250], "target": [-40, 8, 120]}','Aethelred returned from exile in 1014 after Swein''s death and recovered his kingdom; London was central to that. Norse court poetry composed for Olaf Haraldsson credits him with fighting at London and, in one stanza, with breaking ''Lundúna bryggjur''. About two centuries later Snorri Sturluson built these verses into a detailed story of Olaf''s ships pulling the bridge down. No English source mentions such an attack. The bridge here is not shown falling.','conjectural','low','locations/london/episodes.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','london','brentford-1016','Battle of Brentford',1016,0.5,'Summer 1016; day and month not recorded. In the Chronicle''s sequence it follows Cnut''s siege of London (begun about May), Edmund''s fights at Penselwood and Sherston (after midsummer) and his relief of London, with Brentford ''two days'' after the relief. It precedes Assandun (18 October 1016).',51.4805,-0.3075,0,'About 15 km west of the modelled area, up the Thames.','Poorly known. The Chronicle says only that Edmund crossed the Thames at Brentford. The Victoria County History notes the place-name is usually taken to mean the ford over the Brent, though a Thames crossing is possible, and that stakes on the foreshore at ''Old England'' (near the Brent-Thames confluence, by Syon) may have defended a ford; it places the 1016 fight on the south bank. The point given is the approximate Brent-Thames confluence and is a placeholder, not a located battlefield. No archaeology of the battle is known.',NULL,'In 1016 Cnut''s army besieged London, digging a ditch on the south side and dragging its ships to the west of the bridge. Edmund Ironside relieved the city, and two days later crossed the Thames at Brentford and fought the Danish army, putting it to flight. Many of the English were lost by drowning, which the Chronicle blames on their own carelessness in going ahead of the army for plunder. Edmund then went to Wessex to raise more men and the Danes returned to besiege London.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/london/episodes.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','london','barnet-1471','Battle of Barnet',1471,0.28,'Easter Sunday, 14 April 1471 (Julian calendar). Secure. Edward left London about 4 pm on 13 April and deployed in the dark; fighting began around dawn. Warkworth says about 4 am to 10 am; other accounts imply a shorter fight.',51.668,-0.199,0,'About 17 km north-north-west of the modelled area, on the Great North Road.','The general area is certain (north of High Barnet along the old St Albans road); the exact battlefield is not. English Heritage registered a battlefield in 1995 centred on Hadley Green / Monken Hadley, following a National Army Museum report. The 2015-18 Barnet Battlefield Project (University of Huddersfield, with the Battlefields Trust and Barnet Museum) concluded ''we cannot accurately identify the battlefield'': sources agree it lay between half a mile and a mile north of the town, the terrain of the former Kicks End (Kitts End) Common about 1.9 km north of the Barnet boundary best fits them, and Hadley Green is ''problematic''; but metal-detecting found no conclusive battle scatter. Watson (2022) gives the current best guess as a circle of 1 km radius centred on Kitts End. The point given is near Hadley Highstone, between the registered area and Kitts End; treat it as the middle of a zone roughly 2 km long, not a site.',NULL,'Edward IV, returned from exile, marched out of London on 13 April 1471 and deployed at night close to the army of the Earl of Warwick north of Barnet. The armies fought at dawn on Easter Sunday in thick mist, their lines misaligned so that each overlapped the other at one end. Edward won; Warwick and his brother John Neville, Marquess Montagu, were killed. Their bodies were displayed at St Paul''s, and Edward went on to defeat Margaret of Anjou''s army at Tewkesbury on 4 May.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/london/episodes.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','stonehenge','stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','Midsummer sunrise along the axis, c. 2500 BCE',-2500,0.47,'Late June (about 21 June by the solar year) in the sarsen-circle era. The year is a representative one inside 2580 to 2400 BCE; the day is the solstice by astronomy, not a recorded date.',51.178868,-1.826196,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [-2, 43.96, 2], "target": [137.69, 36.8, -115.94]}','Looking from inside the circle north-east along the monument''s axis, past the Heel Stone and down the first stretch of the Avenue, towards where the sun comes up on the longest day. The scene''s one fixed sun is NOT moved: the white line and sun disc are an overlay marking the computed direction (azimuth about 48 to 49 degrees), not real lighting. For 2500 BCE, with the sun''s upper edge just visible and standard refraction, the sunrise point is at azimuth 48.2 degrees on a flat horizon or 49.2 degrees if the horizon is raised 0.6 degrees. The Avenue''s first straight stretch leaves the stones at about 49.9 degrees (from OpenStreetMap mapping), and the Heel Stone lies at about 51 degrees from the centre, so the sun rose slightly to the left of both. The true horizon height to the north-east was not taken from any source read.','reconstructed','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','stonehenge','stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','Midwinter sunset through the Great Trilithon, c. 2500 BCE',-2500,0.98,'Late December (about 21 December by the solar year) in the sarsen-circle era; a representative year inside 2580 to 2400 BCE, the day fixed by astronomy.',51.178868,-1.826196,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [84.14, 42.59, -70.85], "target": [-4.97, 44.02, 4.19]}','From the Avenue, 110 m north-east of the centre, looking south-west through the circle at the Great Trilithon on the day of the shortest sun. As in the sunrise episode the scene''s single fixed sun is not moved: the line and disc are an overlay marking the computed sunset direction. For 2500 BCE, upper edge just visible with standard refraction, the sun set at azimuth 231.0 degrees on a flat horizon or 230.0 degrees if the horizon is raised 0.6 degrees, within about a degree of the reverse of the Avenue''s first stretch (229.9 degrees). Ruggles favours midwinter as the more important of the two solstices, and Parker Pearson cites the midwinter feasting at Durrington Walls; that is interpretation. The horizon height to the south-west was not measured from any source read.','reconstructed','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','stonehenge','stonehenge-raising-sarsens','Raising the sarsens',-2500,0.5,'c. 2560 to 2470 BCE in the model; the sarsen circle is dated 2580 to 2475 BCE and the trilithons 2585 to 2400 BCE (93%) by the English Heritage Bayesian model. The day is arbitrary.',51.178868,-1.826196,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [42, 53.28, 42], "target": [0, 42.92, 0]}','The great sarsen stones go up. The sarsen circle (30 uprights carrying a ring of 30 lintels) and the five trilithons are built over perhaps a century; the model shows the circle part-way through. The stones were dragged from the Marlborough Downs, about 25 km north, and shaped on site; the lintels were fitted with mortise-and-tenon and tongue-and-groove joints borrowed from carpentry. How they were moved and raised is not known: the stones rising smoothly from the ground is the site''s way of showing ''under construction'', not a method.','reconstructed','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','stonehenge','durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','Midwinter feasting at Durrington Walls, c. 2500 BCE',-2500,0.98,'Late December in a representative year of the village''s decade or so of use (c. 2515 to 2480 BCE in the model). The village is dated 2600 to 2500 BCE (2007 release) and, by later modelling, to roughly 10 to 12 years ending 2500 to 2460 BCE.',51.191369,-1.784763,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [3120, 78.46, -1080], "target": [2880, 23.52, -1400]}','A village of small square houses with clay floors stands inside the great timber ring at Durrington Walls, about 2.8 km east of Stonehenge on the Avon. Pig teeth from the site point to animals slaughtered in the winter, and the Southern Circle there is oriented to the midwinter sunrise; the usual reading is that people came here for midwinter feasts and, after the village was abandoned, the timber ring was replaced by a henge bank. How many houses there were and how many people came is not known, and none are drawn.','reconstructed','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','stonehenge','bush-barrow-burial','Bush Barrow burial, c. 1900 BCE',-1900,0.5,'Wikipedia gives c. 1950 BCE for the burial and the Wilsford G7 group 2000 to 1800 BCE; 1900 BCE is a display year inside the uncertainty.',51.170513,-1.834741,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [-520, 66.78, 840], "target": [-596, 46.5, 929]}','Bush Barrow, on the Normanton Down ridge about 1.1 km south-south-west of Stonehenge, is a large round barrow about 49 m across and 3.3 m high, holds a rich Early Bronze Age burial with gold, dated about 1950 BCE. The gold lozenge plate measures about 184 by 156 mm. Round barrows of this period fill the ridges around the stones; here the scene is the barrow standing new on the skyline. Who was buried there is not known from the research notes.','attested-excavated','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','stonehenge','amesbury-abbey-979','Amesbury Abbey founded, 979',979,0.5,'c. 979. The foundation date is approximate; the day is arbitrary.',51.171889,-1.784892,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [2650, 54.76, 1000], "target": [2926, 7.57, 776]}','A Benedictine abbey is founded at Amesbury, in the Avon valley east of Stonehenge, by Queen Aelfthryth. It later becomes a Fontevraud priory (1177) and is dissolved about 1540. What it looked like in 979 and where exactly it stood within Amesbury are not known from the sources read; the precinct on the map is a conjectural rectangle by the church, and the buildings are placeholders.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','stonehenge','inigo-jones-survey-1620','Inigo Jones surveys Stonehenge for James I, 1620',1620,0.6,'1620; the fraction 0.6 is an arbitrary display value, no month is known from the sources read. The survey and the Duke of Buckingham''s digging are both given as 1620, though English Heritage also says ''the 1620s''.',51.178868,-1.826196,1,NULL,NULL,'{"position": [-46, 46.86, 46], "target": [0, 42.92, 0]}','King James I, visiting Wilton House in 1620, asks the architect Inigo Jones to survey the stones. Jones''s plan (published by his pupil John Webb in 1655) shows an idealised, regular monument which he reads as a Roman temple. The same year the Duke of Buckingham has the centre dug, the first known excavation there. The view shows the stones as they are thought to have stood, with the great trilithon down and the south-west trilithon still standing; which stones stood or lay in 1625 is largely inferred. Jones''s plan is a restoration, not a record.','attested-documentary','medium','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "episode"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year","fraction","date_note","lat","lon","in_map","off_map","location_note","view_json","summary","evidence","confidence","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/robin-hoods-ball-3550bce','stonehenge','robin-hoods-ball-3550bce','Robin Hood''s Ball causewayed enclosure, c. 3550 BCE',-3550,0.5,'''Centuries around 3500 BC'' (English Heritage); Wikipedia: probably built c. 4000 BC, possibly used to c. 3000 BC. Low confidence.',51.2128,-1.855,0,'Robin Hood''s Ball lies about 3.8 km north and 2 km west of the stones, 1 km beyond the north edge of this map, so it cannot be shown here. It is an earlier Neolithic causewayed enclosure with two ditch circuits and low banks, never comprehensively excavated.',NULL,NULL,'One of the first lasting monuments of the Stonehenge landscape, a causewayed enclosure on high ground north of the later Cursus, built when the ditches were fresh white chalk. Its size, internal features and end date are not known.','attested-excavated','low','locations/stonehenge/episodes.json','$[7]');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',0,'Date, commanders and outcome: Edward IV, with his brothers Gloucester and Clarence and Lord Hastings, defeated Warwick, Montagu, Oxford and Exeter. Warwick and Montagu were killed, Exeter was left for dead, Oxford escaped.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',1,'Mist: the Arrivall speaks of ''a greate myste'' that stopped either side seeing the other; Warkworth independently says neither could see the other perfectly.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',2,'Misaligned lines: the Arrivall says the hosts did not meet ''directly frount to frount''. At the west end Warwick''s line overreached the king''s and drove it back, with many fleeing ''towards Barnet, and so forthe to London''; at the east end the king''s line overreached theirs.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',3,'The night before: Edward would not let his men stay in the town, lodged them much nearer the enemy than he supposed and kept silence; Warwick''s guns fired through the night and ''alway ovarshote the Kyngs hoste''.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',4,'Warwick arrived first and chose the ground; the Arrivall has his people drawn up ''undre an hedge-syde'' rather more than half a mile from Barnet. Other accounts say a mile, on a ''plain''.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',5,'A chapel for the dead was built afterwards on or near the field (Great Chronicle of London, about 1512).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','attested',6,'Survey 2015–18: systematic metal-detecting, mainly in Wrotham Park, found no clear battle scatter. A few lead round shot found earlier in the Wrotham Park, Kitts End and Old Fold area are probably from the battle. No mass grave has been found.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',0,'Army sizes. The Arrivall (the king''s side) gives 9,000 against 30,000; Warkworth gives Warwick 20,000. Modern figures of about 10,000–12,000 Yorkists against about 15,000 Lancastrians (Battlefields Trust; Barnet Museum gives 10,000–13,000 and 13,000–15,000) are estimates.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',1,'The textbook deployment (Lancastrians: Oxford on the right, Montagu in the centre, Exeter on the left, Warwick in reserve; Yorkists: Hastings left, Edward centre, Gloucester right) is a modern reconstruction, not something a contemporary wrote down.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',2,'Oxford''s badge of a star with streams being mistaken for Edward''s sun with streams, and the cry of ''Treason'': from Warkworth alone.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',3,'Casualties: Warkworth says 4,000; other figures run from about 1,000 to 10,000. None is reliable.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',4,'''Dead Man''s Bottom'': a name for the valley running from Bentley Heath towards Monken Mead, marked in slightly different places on 17th-century and modern maps. The report notes it may have nothing to do with the battle.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','tradition',5,'The Hadley Highstone (an obelisk of 1740, later moved) marks a traditional spot for Warwick''s death; there is no evidence for it.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',0,'Where the battle was fought within a zone about 2 km long from Hadley Green to Kitts End and beyond: the survey report weighs six possible deployments and concludes that the battlefield cannot yet be identified.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',1,'The position, length and facing of both lines, and where each commander stood.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',2,'The real size of either army.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',3,'Which hedge the ''hedge-side'' was, and how much of the ground was open common and how much hedged closes in 1471.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',4,'Where Warwick and Montagu died, and where the dead were buried.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',5,'The number and position of guns on either side.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('barnet/barnet-1471','unknown',6,'How dense the mist was, and for how long; the mist shown is a rendering choice.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',0,'The only narrative source is the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle for 1016: Edmund ''went over at Brentford; where he fought with the enemy, and put them to flight: but there many of the English were drowned, from their own carelessness; who went before the main army with a design to plunder''.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',1,'Just before, in the same entry: Edmund gathered his force a third time, went to London ''all by north of the Thames, and so out through Clayhanger'', relieved the citizens and drove the enemy to their ships. Brentford followed ''within two nights''.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',2,'Just after: Edmund went into Wessex to gather his army, and the Danes soon returned to London and attacked it by land and water.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',3,'Later in the same year Edmund, with a fourth levy, ''forded over the Thames at Brentford'' and went into Kent. The Thames could therefore be waded at Brentford by an army, at least at some states of the river.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',4,'Brentford was an established, named place: recorded from 705, in connection with a meeting of the kings of Wessex and Essex, and the seat of a council under Offa in 780 and a church synod in 781. Half of a Thames fishery there was granted to Winchester in 996.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','attested',5,'The county history judges that the lowest permanent ford on the Thames was probably at Old England, the low ground at the Brent mouth, and (citing Stenton) that the battle was fought on the south bank.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',0,'That the fight was on the Surrey bank after a crossing from north to south: an inference from the order of words in the Chronicle. A poem in praise of Cnut has been read as implying fighting on both banks (reported by Wikipedia; not checked here).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',1,'That the English dead fell in the fighting and were not drowned: argued by Frank Stenton and Russell Poole, as reported by Wikipedia (not checked here).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',2,'That the crossing was at Old England, and that timber stakes found on the foreshore there defended the ford. The stakes are undated. The older claim that they are the stakes Julius Caesar met when he forced the Thames in 54 BCE is antiquarian tradition; Mortimer Wheeler doubted it in 1929.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',3,'That Cnut was ''twice driven back across the Thames at Brentford'', as a local history page puts it. The Chronicle records one fight at Brentford and one later crossing by Edmund on his way into Kent.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',4,'A Danish strength of 8,000 to 12,000, given in Wikipedia''s summary box, has no contemporary basis.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','tradition',5,'The granite monument at Brentford that commemorates Caesar''s crossing and the battle marks tradition, not a located site.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',0,'Where the army crossed, and on which bank or banks the fighting took place.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',1,'The day and month.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',2,'Numbers on either side, and losses. The figures in the model are stand-ins and are not to scale with any army.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',3,'Whether Cnut was there in person, and who led the Danes on the day.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',4,'Where, when and how the drowning happened: at the crossing, in pursuit, or somewhere else on the river.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',5,'The state of the tide and the river, the weather, and whether the Danes had ships at hand.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',6,'What stood at Brentford in 1016. No late Saxon building has been excavated there.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('brentford/brentford-1016','unknown',7,'The exact form of the river: its width, the shoals and islands, and the mouths of the Brent.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',0,'Tacitus (Annals 14.33) is the only ancient writer to name Londinium in this context. He calls it a busy centre of merchants and goods, says Suetonius marched there, considered making it his base, decided to sacrifice the one town to save the province, and left; those who stayed behind were killed. Verulamium suffered the same.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',1,'Tacitus says Londinium did not have the status of a colonia. It was a trading town about a decade or so old.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',2,'There was no defence of the town: the governor abandoned it. No pitched fight at London is described in any source.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',3,'A widespread layer of burnt red clay (daub), charred wood and debris, containing pre-60 coins and samian pottery, is recorded on City sites between the Walbrook and the bridgehead. MOLA treats the ''Boudican destruction of the settlement in AD 60'' as an event documented by both archaeology and text, and uses it as a dating horizon.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',4,'Burning of this date has also been found in buildings beside the Roman road at the south end of the bridge in Southwark (reported from the mid-1990s onward), so the destruction is taken to have crossed the river.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',5,'Buildings of the period were timber and clay (wattle-and-daub or mud-brick) and mostly low; burnt daub is the main component of the layer.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','attested',6,'Recovery: a stylus tablet from the Bloomberg site records a contract dated 21 October AD 62 to bring twenty loads of provisions from Verulamium to London; a fort of about 3.7 acres with earth-and-timber ramparts and double ditches was built at Plantation Place (Fenchurch Street) in about AD 63.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',0,'Casualties: Tacitus gives ''close upon seventy thousand'' Roman citizens and allies killed across the places attacked (Camulodunum, Londinium, Verulamium together, not London alone). Cassius Dio gives 80,000 and ''two cities'' sacked, without naming London. Both are rhetorical round numbers; the Loeb editor''s note on Tacitus calls the figure probably valueless.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',1,'Atrocities: Dio''s detailed account of mutilation and impalement of captives is not tied to London and has no archaeological support. Dio survives here only in a later epitome and wrote about 150 years after the event (epitome status not verified here).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',2,'Rebel army size: Dio gives 120,000 at the outset and 230,000 at the final battle. Not usable.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',3,'Layer thickness: a secondary website gives ''typically 30-60 cm'' of bright red burnt daub, with pits filled to 1.5 m; the 1928 RCHME survey records the layer at 10-13 ft below modern ground. No primary excavation figure was verified here, and the figure will vary site to site.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',4,'Population of Londinium in AD 60: modern estimates of roughly 9,000-10,000 circulate in popular accounts (seen only in search results, not verified here). There is no ancient figure.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','tradition',5,'Skulls from the Walbrook as massacre victims: an old suggestion repeated in the press; not established.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',0,'The month and season, time of day, weather, and how long the destruction took.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',1,'The direction from which the rebels approached and whether they crossed the bridge or the river to reach Southwark.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',2,'How many people remained in the town and how many died there.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',3,'Whether Boudica was present in person, and what the rebels looked like in detail.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',4,'The exact street plan and building-by-building layout of the pre-revolt town; the form of the bridge or crossing in AD 60.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/boudica-60','unknown',5,'Whether the whole built area burned or only parts of it; the mapped extent in Southwark.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','attested',0,'A fortified timber bridge at London existed at this time: structural timbers are tree-ring dated to c. 987-1032, and the bridge is mentioned in the law code IV Aethelred.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','attested',1,'The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle shows the bridge mattered militarily: in 1013 part of Swein''s army drowned in the Thames because they did not use a bridge, and London held out for Aethelred and Thorkell; in 1016 Cnut''s ships could not pass it.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','attested',2,'Aethelred returned in Lent 1014 and then drove Cnut out of Lindsey (Anglo-Saxon Chronicle).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','attested',3,'Near-contemporary skaldic verse exists: Ottarr svarti''s Hofudlausn, stanza 7, addresses Olaf with ''you broke the bryggjur of London''; Sigvatr Thordarson''s Vikingarvisur lists a battle of Olaf''s at London and Sudvirki (Southwark). These are praise poems by Olaf''s own poets.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','attested',4,'Olaf was venerated in London soon afterwards: six medieval churches in London and Southwark were dedicated to him, one at the south end of the bridge (Tooley Street is a corruption of St Olave''s).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','tradition',0,'The whole narrative of the attack comes from Snorri''s Heimskringla (Saga of St Olaf, written c. 1230): Danes holding London and a fortification at Southwark with large ditches and a rampart of timber, stone and turf; a bridge wide enough for two wagons to pass, with towers and breast-high wooden parapets on the downstream side, standing on piles.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','tradition',1,'The tactics are Snorri''s alone: Olaf roofed his ships with platforms made from pulled-down houses (wood bound with hazel or withies, often rendered ''wattle'' or ''wickerwork'' in modern retellings) on stout posts, rowed up under the bridge under a hail of stones and missiles, fastened cables round the piles, and rowed hard downstream until the piles loosened and the loaded bridge gave way; Southwark was then stormed and London submitted to Aethelred.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','tradition',2,'That ''bryggjur'' means the bridge: the word is plural and can mean wharves or jetties. The editor of Sigvatr''s poem argues that his stanza refers to London''s wharves, not its bridge.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','tradition',3,'That Olaf fought for Aethelred: this is Snorri''s framing; the Encyclopaedia Romana page notes other sources put Olaf on the Danish side.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','tradition',4,'The link to the nursery rhyme ''London Bridge is falling down'': Samuel Laing''s 1844 translation was phrased to echo the rhyme; John Clark''s study rejects the folk-memory idea.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','unknown',0,'Whether any attack on the bridge happened at all, and if so in which year and on which side Olaf fought.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','unknown',1,'Whether the bridge was pulled down, damaged, or simply fought over. Hagland and Watson, who think the attack plausible, say the damage may well be exaggerated.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','unknown',2,'The actual form of the bridge superstructure and of any Southwark defences in 1014; no excavated evidence fixes them for this date.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','unknown',3,'Numbers of ships and men, the defenders'' identity, the season, weather and tide.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/london-bridge-1014','unknown',4,'Whether 9th-11th-century axes, spearheads and a grappling iron dredged near the bridge relate to fighting at all (they may be carpenters'' losses).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','attested',0,'The only narrative source is the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle entry for 1016: Edmund ''went over at Brentford'', fought the army and put it to flight; many English drowned through their own carelessness, having gone ahead of the main force hoping for booty.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','attested',1,'Context in the same entry: the Danish ships came to Greenwich and then London, ''dug a great ditch on the south side and dragged their ships to the west side of the bridge'', then enclosed the city with a ditch so that no one could get in or out; the city held.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','attested',2,'The bridge was held against Cnut in 1016, which is why the ships had to be taken round it through north Southwark.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','attested',3,'After the battle Edmund went to Wessex to gather the army and the Danes resumed the siege of London; later in the year Edmund ''forded over the Thames at Brentford'' a second time on his way into Kent.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','attested',4,'An anonymous skaldic poem, Lidsmannaflokkr, in the voice of a Danish soldier, celebrates fighting by the Thames and beside the dyke at London in this campaign (it does not describe Brentford).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','tradition',0,'That the battle was fought on the south (Surrey) bank after a crossing from north to south: an inference from the Chronicle''s wording, repeated by the Victoria County History. Wikipedia reports that Knutsdrapa has been read as implying fighting on both banks (lead only, not verified here).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','tradition',1,'That the English dead were battle casualties, not drowned: argued by Frank Stenton and Russell Poole according to Wikipedia (lead only, not verified here).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','tradition',2,'That the crossing was at Old England by the Brent mouth, and that the foreshore stakes there relate to a defended ford: possible, unproven, and the stakes are undated in the source read.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','tradition',3,'The modern Brentford monument listing the battle marks tradition, not a located site.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','tradition',4,'A Danish strength of 8,000-12,000 appears in Wikipedia''s infobox without contemporary basis.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',0,'The date, the exact crossing point, and which bank the fighting was on.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',1,'Whether Cnut was present in person; who commanded the Danes on the day.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',2,'Numbers on either side and losses.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',3,'Where and how the drowning happened (at the ford on the crossing, or in pursuit).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',4,'The course and width of Cnut''s ditch through Southwark; no excavated trace was verified here.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/brentford-1016','unknown',5,'The state of the tide, the weather, and the look of the riverbank (the Thames here was wider, shallower and braided with aits).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',0,'Date, commanders and outcome: Edward IV defeated Warwick; Warwick and Montagu were killed; the Duke of Exeter was badly wounded and left for dead; the Earl of Oxford escaped. Henry VI was brought to the field with Edward''s army and returned to the Tower.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',1,'Mist or fog: reported independently by the Arrivall, Warkworth and Gerhard von Wesel''s newsletter of 17 April 1471 (which says the fog was also in London).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',2,'Misaligned lines: the Arrivall says the two battles did not meet ''directly frount to frount'' because of the mist. At the west end the Lancastrians overreached the king''s line and drove it back, with fugitives fleeing through Barnet towards London; at the east end the king''s line overreached theirs.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',3,'Night before: Edward would not let his men stay in the town, lodged them very close to the enemy and kept silence; Warwick''s guns fired through the night and overshot.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',4,'Warwick had arrived first and chose the ground; the Arrivall has him ''undre an hedge-syde'' somewhat more than half a mile from Barnet; von Wesel and the Great Chronicle of London imply nearer a mile. The battle was fought on foot.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',5,'Yorkist order of march and battle (Great Chronicle of London, Hall): Gloucester the vanguard, Edward with Clarence the main battle, Hastings the rearward. Oxford led the Lancastrian vanguard and routed the wing opposite him, which was Hastings''s.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',6,'A chapel for the dead was established near the field soon after; the hermitage chapel at Kitts End is the usual candidate, but geophysics there found a moat and no graves.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','attested',7,'Survey finds 2015-18: of 1,007 recorded objects only 16-17 were certainly medieval; a few lead round shot are known from the Wrotham Park / Kitts End / Old Fold area. No mass grave has been found.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',0,'Army sizes: the sources contradict each other along party lines. Arrivall (Yorkist): 9,000 against 30,000. Warkworth: Edward about 7,000-8,000 against Warwick 20,000. Von Wesel: Edward 15,000-20,000. Polydore Vergil (decades later): Edward ''far superior'' in numbers. Modern compromise figures (Hammond: about 12,000 Yorkists, 15,000 Lancastrians; others 10,000 and 15,000) are estimates, and the project report notes historians now tend to reduce such numbers further.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',1,'The tidy textbook deployment (Lancastrians: Oxford right, Montagu centre, Exeter left, Warwick in reserve; Yorkists: Hastings left, Edward centre, Gloucester right) is a modern reconstruction. The wing positions are inferred from military convention (vanguard on the right) plus the Arrivall; Hall, the only chronicler to give a Lancastrian order, puts Oxford and Montagu on the left, Warwick and Exeter on the right, and Somerset (who was not there) in the centre.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',2,'Oxford''s star badge mistaken for Edward''s sun and the cry of ''Treason'': from Warkworth alone. The History of Parliament notes it ''is perhaps not to be taken too seriously'' and that there is no evidence a star with streams was a de Vere badge.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',3,'Montagu wearing Edward''s livery and being killed by Warwick''s own men (Warkworth): regarded as improbable.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',4,'Warwick killed fleeing to a wood, or while reaching his horse: details vary by source. The Hadley Highstone obelisk (1740) marks a traditional spot only.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',5,'Casualties: Sir John Paston''s letter says over 1,000 besides the notables; Warkworth about 4,000; a foreign report 10,000; von Wesel says more than 10,000 broken arrows lay on the field. None is reliable.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','tradition',6,'Place-names ''Gladmore Heath'' and ''Dead Man''s Bottom'': the project report shows ''Gladmore Heath'' grew out of a map label (''Gladmore heath field'') on Norden''s 1598 map, and treats Dead Man''s Bottom as a late, possibly spurious association.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',0,'The exact position, length and orientation of both battle lines (probably roughly east-west across or beside the St Albans road, but six different deployments are considered possible in the project report).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',1,'The real size of either army and of each division, and where Warwick, Montagu and Exeter each stood.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',2,'Where Warwick and Montagu died.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',3,'The number, type and position of guns on either side.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',4,'How far the landscape was open heath or hedged enclosure in 1471 (the commons were probably larger than later maps show).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('london/barnet-1471','unknown',5,'How long the fighting lasted and how dense the mist was through the morning.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','attested',0,'The monument''s main axis runs north-east to south-west, shared by the trilithon horseshoe, the bluestone settings, the sarsen circle and the first stretch of the Avenue (Darvill et al. 2012).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','attested',1,'Midsummer sunrise is seen over and slightly to the left of the Heel Stone and falls within the Avenue (Sky at Night).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','attested',2,'The first straight stretch of the Avenue leaves the stones at azimuth about 49.9 degrees, measured from OpenStreetMap-mapped earthwork outlines (this project''s geography research, not a survey azimuth).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','attested',3,'William Stukeley recognised the solstice axis in 1720.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','tradition',0,'That the builders meant midsummer sunrise: Ruggles favours midwinter as the more important; Hawkins''s 1960s claims are generally treated as over-optimistic.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','tradition',1,'That crowds gathered at the stones on this morning in 2500 BCE; the figures are not shown.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','unknown',0,'What, if anything, was done at the stones on midsummer morning, and whether the alignment was meant to be precise to a degree.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','unknown',1,'The altitude of the true horizon north-east of the stones (woodland, relief): the computed range uses a flat 0 degree and a 0.6 degree horizon as an assumption.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','unknown',2,'Today the same sunrise is about 1 degree larger in azimuth (49.2 degrees on a flat horizon) because the Earth''s axial tilt has fallen from about 24.0 to 23.4 degrees.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midsummer-sunrise-2500bce','unknown',3,'Computed with cos A = (sin d - sin phi sin h) / (cos phi cos h), latitude 51.179 degrees, obliquity 23.97 degrees (Laskar series, 2500 BCE), refraction 0.57 degrees, solar semi-diameter 0.27 degrees: my calculation, not a source.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','attested',0,'From the far end of the Avenue the midwinter sunset can be seen through the central arches (Sky at Night).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','attested',1,'The Avenue''s first straight stretch, the trilithon horseshoe and the circles share one axis, open to the north-east and facing the Great Trilithon in the south-west (Darvill et al. 2012).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','attested',2,'Durrington Walls pig-tooth evidence points to midwinter slaughter and feasting (cited by Parker Pearson in the Sky at Night account).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','tradition',0,'That the monument was built to frame the midwinter sunset (Ruggles''s preferred reading; Darvill argues the trilithon layout points to a midwinter focus). Midsummer sunrise as the main intent is the older, popular view.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','tradition',1,'That people gathered on this evening; none are drawn.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','unknown',0,'What ceremonies took place, and whether the alignment was precise to better than a degree.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','unknown',1,'The altitude of the south-west horizon, so the exact azimuth range: 230.0 to 231.0 degrees assumes a 0 to 0.6 degree horizon, my assumption.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-midwinter-sunset-2500bce','unknown',2,'Today the sunset is about 1 degree larger in azimuth (231.0 degrees flat horizon) than in 2500 BCE. Computed as in the sunrise episode; my calculation, not a source.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','attested',0,'A sarsen circle of 30 uprights and 30 lintels, outer diameter about 29.6 m, was designed (Darvill et al. 2012); the uprights are about 4.1 m high, the lintel tops about 4.9 m above ground.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','attested',1,'Five trilithons form a horseshoe about 13.7 m across, open to the north-east; the tallest stone (56) stands about 6.7 m above ground with about 2.4 m buried.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','attested',2,'Radiocarbon dating of an antler in the socket of stones 53 and 54 gives 2585 to 2400 BCE (93%).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','attested',3,'The sarsens come from the Marlborough Downs about 25 km north (English Heritage; Nash et al. 2020, from a search summary only).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','tradition',0,'Geoffrey of Monmouth''s tale that Merlin brought the stones from Ireland (1136) is legend.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','unknown',0,'How the stones were transported and raised, how many people it took and how long it took.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','unknown',1,'Whether the sarsen circle was ever completed: stones 13, 17, 18, 20 and 24 and 24 lintels are missing today. Ashbee argued it was never finished; Darvill thinks it was robbed; parchmarks from 2013 hint at a complete circle. Unresolved.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/stonehenge-raising-sarsens','unknown',2,'The order of the trilithons, circle and bluestone settings within Stage 2.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','attested',0,'Eight houses about 5 m (16 ft) square, with clay floors, central hearths and box-bed and dresser outlines, were excavated in 2006.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','attested',1,'Occupation was brief, about 10 to 12 years, with chalk floors renewed at most nine times (Bayesian modelling).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','attested',2,'Pig-tooth evidence points to midwinter slaughter (cited by Parker Pearson in the Sky at Night account); the Southern Circle is oriented to midwinter sunrise (Wikipedia).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','attested',3,'Up to about 300 timber posts in a ring 440 to 450 m across stood under the later bank; they were pulled about 2480 BCE.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','tradition',0,'That Durrington''s occupants were connected with building or using Stonehenge: widely suggested, not proven by the sources read.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','tradition',1,'That feasting here was tied to a midwinter gathering at Stonehenge, ''up to 4,000 people'' being an upper-end speculation.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','unknown',0,'The number of houses and people (a few dozen houses are drawn, conjecturally), and exactly where in the henge they stood.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','unknown',1,'What the timber ring and the circles were for; whether the Avenue to the river was walked at this season.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/durrington-midwinter-feast-2500bce','unknown',2,'The height of the posts (5 m per the 2016 excavations, 7 m per Wikipedia: conflict).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','attested',0,'Bush Barrow lies on Normanton Down, 3.3 m high and 49 m overall (or 40 m wide by another count).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','attested',1,'It held an Early Bronze Age burial with a gold lozenge about 184 x 156 x 1 mm, dated about 1950 BCE.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','attested',2,'Normanton Down has about 40 round barrows (bowl, bell, disc and a saucer), of which at least 35 were surviving in 1957, with burials between about 2600 and 1600 BCE.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','tradition',0,'That the burial was a chieftain''s or king''s: ''chieftain'' is a modern label.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','tradition',1,'That the barrows were meant to be seen from the stones, or the reverse.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','unknown',0,'The buried person''s name, status or relation to Stonehenge, and the exact date of the burial.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','unknown',1,'The barrow''s original form: its present shape is modern survey, and the model''s ''new'' mound is generic.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/bush-barrow-burial','unknown',2,'The other grave goods are not itemised in the research notes for this project.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','attested',0,'A Benedictine abbey was founded at Amesbury c. 979 by Queen Aelfthryth (Wikipedia).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','attested',1,'It was refounded as a Fontevraud priory in 1177 and dissolved c. 1540; the estate went to the Seymours.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','attested',2,'The parish church of St Mary and St Melor may be the surviving priory church (Wikipedia; unproven).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','unknown',0,'The site, size and form of the 10th-century buildings, and the precinct''s boundary.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/amesbury-abbey-979','unknown',1,'Whether the Dissolution date is 1539 or 1540: sources conflict.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','attested',0,'Jones surveyed Stonehenge for James I in the 1620s (English Heritage); Webb''s 1655 publication reports the king''s commission after his visit to Wilton in 1620.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','attested',1,'The Duke of Buckingham''s digging in the centre is the first known excavation there (English Heritage).');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','attested',2,'Stone 55 and the Great Trilithon had fallen before the first 17th-century plans (Darvill); the south-west trilithon (57 and 58 with lintel 158) stood until 1797.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','tradition',0,'Jones''s claim that Stonehenge was a Roman temple: now rejected.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','unknown',0,'Which other stones were fallen, leaning or broken in 1625: no dated record; the model uses inference from later plans.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/inigo-jones-survey-1620','unknown',1,'How accurately Jones measured: his plan is an ideal geometry and cannot be used as a measured survey.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/robin-hoods-ball-3550bce','attested',0,'A causewayed enclosure with two ditch circuits and low banks, with one causeway noted by Wikipedia.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/robin-hoods-ball-3550bce','attested',1,'Dated to the centuries around 3500 BCE by English Heritage.');
INSERT INTO "episode_fact"("episode_id","kind","seq","text") VALUES('stonehenge/robin-hoods-ball-3550bce','unknown',0,'Its diameter, internal features and the date it went out of use.');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/before-the-battle','barnet','before-the-battle','The ridge before the battle',1400,1470,1450,'A small market town on its hilltop, the main road from London to St Albans and the north running across open greens and commons, a moated manor at Old Fold, a small church at Hadley on the edge of Enfield Chase. Barnet''s chapel is rebuilt in these decades. How much of the land was hedged closes and how much was open heath is only partly known.','locations/barnet/eras.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/battle-and-after','barnet','battle-and-after','The battle and after',1471,1500,1471,'Easter Sunday, 14 April 1471: Edward IV and the Earl of Warwick fight at dawn in thick mist somewhere on this ridge. The two shaded zones and the blocks of figures appear for 1471 only and mark possibilities, not a located battlefield. A chapel for the dead follows, on a site still not proven, and Hadley church is rebuilt about 1494.','locations/barnet/eras.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/late-saxon','brentford','late-saxon','Late Anglo-Saxon Brentford',950,1065,1016,'A named crossing place on the road west from London, where the Brent meets the Thames. Kings met here in 705 and church councils in 780 and 781, and half a Thames fishery at Brentford was granted away in 996, but no late Saxon building has been excavated. In 1016 Edmund Ironside crossed the Thames here twice in his war with Cnut.','locations/brentford/eras.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/early-norman','brentford','early-norman','After the Conquest',1066,1100,1086,'Brentford is not named in Domesday Book (1086); its ground lay within the manors of Isleworth, Hanwell and the bishop of London''s Fulham. Nothing is recorded as changing here in these years, and the scene is left as it was.','locations/brentford/eras.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/prehistoric','london','prehistoric','Prehistoric Thames',-4000,42,-1500,'A wide, braided tidal river between gravel terraces and marsh. Woodland is cleared gradually from the Bronze Age. No town stands here; evidence for settlement on the site of the later City is slight.','locations/london/eras.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/roman','london','roman','Roman Londinium',43,410,150,'A new town founded around 47–50 CE beside a bridge. Burned in 60/61, rebuilt with a forum, amphitheatre, fort and quays, walled around 200, then shrinking through the 3rd and 4th centuries.','locations/london/eras.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/saxon','london','saxon','Post-Roman & Anglo-Saxon',411,1065,780,'The walled city is largely empty for generations. A trading settlement, Lundenwic, grows to the west along the Strand; from 886 settlement moves back inside the Roman walls.','locations/london/eras.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/medieval','london','medieval','Norman & Medieval London',1066,1484,1400,'The Tower, a vast cathedral, a stone bridge lined with houses, over a hundred parish churches and many religious houses. Westminster grows as a separate royal centre.','locations/london/eras.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/tudor','london','tudor','Tudor London',1485,1602,1575,'Rapid growth beyond the walls. The monasteries are dissolved and converted, St Paul''s loses its spire (1561), the Royal Exchange opens and playhouses rise on Bankside.','locations/london/eras.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/jacobean','london','jacobean','Jacobean London',1603,1625,1616,'The city of James I: a crowded timber metropolis of roughly 200,000 or more, with the first strict classical building, the Banqueting House (1619–22), at Whitehall.','locations/london/eras.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/earlier-neolithic','stonehenge','earlier-neolithic','Earlier Neolithic landscape',-4000,-3001,-3500,'Before any stone is raised. Woodland is thinning into open chalk grassland. Robin Hood''s Ball (just outside this map), long barrows and, by about 3500 BCE, the Greater Cursus mark the first lasting monuments; the Mesolithic pine posts near the later stones are older still and not shown. Dates are approximate and several rest on a single antler.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/first-stonehenge','stonehenge','first-stonehenge','First Stonehenge',-3000,-2621,-2850,'Stage 1 in Darvill et al.''s chronology: a circular ditch and bank about 110 m across with a north-east and a smaller southern entrance, 56 Aubrey Holes (possibly holding the first bluestones, which is argued over) and many cremation burials. Timber structures are inferred from postholes. The model switches the ditch on about 3000 to 2900 BCE; the exact date is a range.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/sarsens-durrington','stonehenge','sarsens-durrington','Sarsens and Durrington',-2620,-2281,-2500,'Stages 2 and 3. The trilithons, the sarsen circle and the first bluestone settings go up, c. 2620 to 2480 BCE; the Heel Stone, Slaughter Stone and Station Stones belong here too, with uncertain order. At Durrington Walls a village stands for a decade or so and a great timber circle is replaced by the henge bank, then Woodhenge. The Avenue is laid out in this era. Whether the sarsen circle was ever completed is unresolved.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/beaker-bronze-age','stonehenge','beaker-bronze-age','Beaker and Early Bronze Age',-2280,-1501,-1900,'Stages 4 and 5. The bluestones are rearranged into an oval and a circle; the Y and Z holes are dug; axe and dagger carvings are made on the sarsens. Beaker burials appear (the Amesbury Archer, c. 2340 to 2300 BCE, lies outside this map). Round barrow cemeteries multiply on the ridges, among them Normanton Down and Bush Barrow.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/later-prehistory','stonehenge','later-prehistory','Later prehistory',-1500,42,-600,'Almost nothing is recorded at the stones themselves after the last stone settings (about 1500 BCE). Fields and enclosures spread; the land is largely cleared by 800 to 500 BCE; Vespasian''s Camp hillfort is built, with a placeholder date of about 700 to 600 BCE. The model''s stone states stay as left.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/roman-medieval','stonehenge','roman-medieval','Roman to medieval',43,1499,979,'Roman coins and pottery at the stones (late 4th century), a 7th-century execution burial, Amesbury Abbey (founded c. 979, refounded as a priory in 1177) and the first written accounts of the stones (Henry of Huntingdon c. 1130, Geoffrey of Monmouth c. 1136). No dated event changes the stones; which stood or lay by 1625 is inferred from later drawings and plans.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/tudor-jacobean','stonehenge','tudor-jacobean','Tudor and Jacobean rediscovery',1500,1625,1620,'The priory is dissolved c. 1540 and the first drawings of the stones appear (de Heere 1573 to 75), then Camden''s Britannia (1586) and, in 1620, Inigo Jones''s survey for James I and the Duke of Buckingham''s dig. These are the earliest views that can anchor the state of the stones.','locations/stonehenge/eras.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/neolithic','england-wales','neolithic','Neolithic: first farmers and great monuments',-4000,-2201,-2500,'Farming arrives about 4000 BCE. Communities clear patches of woodland, mine flint and quarry stone axes, and build long barrows, causewayed enclosures and, after 3000 BCE, henges and stone circles. There are no towns and few permanent villages.','national/eras.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/bronze-age','england-wales','bronze-age','Bronze Age',-2200,-801,-1500,'Metalworking, round barrows and, from about 1500 BCE, a countryside divided into fields and farmsteads. The great monuments fall out of use; offerings are made in rivers and fens instead.','national/eras.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/iron-age','england-wales','iron-age','Iron Age',-800,42,-100,'Hillforts dominate much of the south and west until about 100 BCE. In the last century before the conquest, coin-issuing kingdoms and large lowland centres (oppida) appear in the south-east, in contact with Roman Gaul. The peoples named on the map are those recorded by Roman writers.','national/eras.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/roman','england-wales','roman','Roman Britain',43,410,150,'Conquest from 43 CE brings forts, roads and a network of towns. The north and west stay under the army behind Hadrian''s Wall. The province is split in two about 200 and into four about 300; towns shrink in the 4th century and Roman government ends about 410.','national/eras.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/early-anglo-saxon','england-wales','early-anglo-saxon','Early Anglo-Saxon kingdoms',411,792,700,'Towns empty. English-speaking kingdoms form in the east and south while British kingdoms hold the west. Christianity returns from Rome (597) and Iona (635); by 700 there are monasteries, bishoprics and a few trading towns (wics), and Mercia is becoming the leading power.','national/eras.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/viking-age','england-wales','viking-age','Viking Age and the making of England',793,1065,900,'Raids from 793, then conquest: the Great Army destroys Northumbria, East Anglia and half of Mercia in 866-877. Wessex survives, fortifies its burhs and takes back the Danelaw. From 927 there is a single kingdom of the English, conquered in turn by Cnut in 1016.','national/eras.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/norman-angevin','england-wales','norman-angevin','Norman and Angevin England',1066,1215,1150,'The Norman Conquest replaces the ruling class and covers the country with castles, new cathedrals and monasteries. Domesday Book (1086) records it. Towns and population grow fast; Norman lords push into Wales and create the March.','national/eras.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/later-medieval','england-wales','later-medieval','Later medieval England',1216,1484,1340,'Population peaks about 1300, with new towns, ports and universities. Then famine (1315-17) and the Black Death (1348-9) roughly halve it. Wales is conquered in 1282-3. War with Scotland and France is followed by the Wars of the Roses.','national/eras.json','$[7]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/tudor','england-wales','tudor','Tudor England and Wales',1485,1602,1575,'The monasteries are dissolved (1536-40) and Wales is joined to England in law (1536-42). Population recovers; London grows far beyond every other town, cloth towns rise and some old cities decay.','national/eras.json','$[8]');
INSERT INTO "era"("id","location_id","local_id","name","year_from","year_to","preset_year","blurb","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/jacobean','england-wales','jacobean','Jacobean England and Wales',1603,1625,1616,'James VI of Scotland inherits the English crown in 1603. About four to five million people, most of them in villages; London holds perhaps one in twenty of them and Norwich, Bristol, York and Newcastle follow far behind.','national/eras.json','$[9]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1453#0','barnet',0,1453,'Barnet chapel rebuilt','John Beauchamp, who died in 1453, rebuilt the nave arcades and clerestory of the chapel of St John the Baptist; the tower is early 15th century and the chancel about 1450. The dates are architectural estimates apart from Beauchamp''s death.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1455#0','barnet',1,1455,'First Battle of St Albans','22 May 1455, about 15 km to the north-west along the same road: the opening battle of the Wars of the Roses. Off this map.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1461#0','barnet',2,1461,'Towton; Edward IV becomes king','After the second battle of St Albans (17 February) Edward of York was proclaimed king in London in March and won at Towton in Yorkshire on 29 March 1461. Off this map.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1470#0','barnet',3,1470,'Henry VI restored by Warwick','The ''readeption'': Warwick, now allied with the Lancastrians, restored Henry VI in October 1470 and Edward IV fled abroad. He landed in Yorkshire in March 1471 and entered London on 11 April.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1471#0','barnet',4,1471,'Battle of Barnet, 14 April','Easter Sunday. Edward IV defeated the Earl of Warwick somewhere between half a mile and a mile north of the town, at dawn in thick mist; Warwick and his brother Montagu were killed. The exact ground has not been found.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1471#1','barnet',5,1471,'Battle of Tewkesbury, 4 May','Three weeks later Edward defeated Margaret of Anjou''s army in Gloucestershire, ending the Lancastrian challenge. Off this map.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1479#0','barnet',6,1479,'Kitts End heath first named','A close roll entry of 1479 names Kitts (Kicks) End heath, Bentley heath and many hedged closes north of the common: the earliest detailed record of the landscape north of Hadley, eight years after the battle.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1485#0','barnet',7,1485,'Bosworth; Henry VII becomes king','22 August 1485, in Leicestershire. Off this map.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[7]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('barnet/1494#0','barnet',8,1494,'Hadley church rebuilt','The church of St Mary at Monken Hadley was rebuilt about 1494, the date carved on its west tower, presumably on the site of an older church.','locations/barnet/events.json','$[8]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/996#0','brentford',0,996,'Half a Thames fishery at Brentford granted to Winchester','King Æthelred granted half of a Thames fishery at Brentford to the church of St Peter and St Paul, Winchester. It is the only record of activity at Brentford found for the half-century before the battle.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1013#0','brentford',1,1013,'Swein''s army loses men crossing the Thames','On its way towards London, the Chronicle says, many of Swein Forkbeard''s army drowned in the Thames ''because they kept not to any bridge''. The place is not given and need not be Brentford; it shows what a crossing of the river could cost.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1016#0','brentford',2,1016,'Cnut besieges London','The Danish ships came to London, dug a ditch on the south side and dragged their ships to the west of the bridge, then shut the city in. Edmund gathered an army, came to London north of the Thames and drove the besiegers to their ships.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1016#1','brentford',3,1016,'Battle of Brentford','Within two nights of relieving London, Edmund ''went over at Brentford'', fought the Danish army and put it to flight. Many of the English drowned ''from their own carelessness'', having gone ahead of the army to plunder. Site, numbers and course are unknown; the figures shown are stand-ins.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1016#2','brentford',4,1016,'Edmund fords the Thames at Brentford again','Later in the year Edmund gathered the English a fourth time, ''forded over the Thames at Brentford'' and went into Kent, driving the Danes to Sheppey. He was beaten at Assandun and died on St Andrew''s day (30 November); Cnut took the whole kingdom.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1066#0','brentford',5,1066,'Norman Conquest','Nothing is recorded at Brentford in the sources read. Isleworth, held by Earl Ælfgar in 1066, had passed to Walter of St Valery by 1086.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('brentford/1086#0','brentford',6,1086,'Domesday Book: Brentford is not named','The ground lay in three manors. Isleworth had 118 households, land for 55 ploughs, two mills and woodland for 500 pigs, and was worth £72 (£80 in 1066). Hanwell, held by Westminster Abbey and assessed at 8 hides, had about 17 households and a mill (figures from a secondary summary). The county history thinks Old Brentford was probably counted within the bishop of London''s manor of Fulham.','locations/brentford/events.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/-1500#0','london',0,-1500,'Bronze Age farming on the Southwark eyots','Plough (ard) marks and timber trackways are known from Southwark and Bermondsey; the cultivation was short-lived and was drowned as water levels rose.','locations/london/events.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/43#0','london',1,43,'Roman invasion of Britain',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/47#0','london',2,47,'Londinium founded (47–50 CE)','London Museum dates the foundation to 47–50 CE; the earliest dated feature is a roadside drain of 47 CE (tree-ring date). There is no documented foundation year.','locations/london/events.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/60#0','london',3,60,'Boudican revolt: Londinium burned','60 or 61 CE. Recovery was quick: a fort and a new quay by 63.','locations/london/events.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/125#0','london',4,125,'Hadrianic fire','A major fire c. 120–130, most likely the mid 120s.','locations/london/events.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/200#0','london',5,200,'City wall built','Landward wall c. 190–225.','locations/london/events.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/410#0','london',6,410,'End of Roman administration',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/604#0','london',7,604,'St Paul''s founded',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[7]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/886#0','london',8,886,'Alfred restores Lundenburh','Settlement returns inside the Roman walls; reoccupation may have begun a generation earlier.','locations/london/events.json','$[8]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1066#0','london',9,1066,'Norman Conquest',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[9]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1087#0','london',10,1087,'Fire destroys St Paul''s',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[10]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1091#0','london',11,1091,'Storm destroys the timber London Bridge','The rebuilding date is not recorded.','locations/london/events.json','$[11]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1135#0','london',12,1135,'Fire burns the timber bridge','1135 or 1136 depending on the source.','locations/london/events.json','$[12]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1209#0','london',13,1209,'Stone London Bridge complete',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[13]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1212#0','london',14,1212,'Great fire of Southwark and the bridge','11 July 1212: Southwark, St Mary Overie and the houses on the new bridge burn.','locations/london/events.json','$[14]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1348#0','london',15,1348,'Black Death','November 1348 to 1349–50; buildings survive but a large part of the population dies.','locations/london/events.json','$[15]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1381#0','london',16,1381,'Peasants'' Revolt: Savoy Palace destroyed',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[16]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1485#0','london',17,1485,'Tudor accession',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[17]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1538#0','london',18,1538,'Dissolution of the London religious houses','1536–40.','locations/london/events.json','$[18]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1561#0','london',19,1561,'St Paul''s spire destroyed by lightning','4 June 1561; the roofs were repaired by 1566 but the spire was never rebuilt.','locations/london/events.json','$[19]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1563#0','london',20,1563,'Plague epidemic','Boulton: plague epidemics in 1563, 1603, 1625 and 1665 each killed between 20 and 25% of Londoners.','locations/london/events.json','$[20]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1577#0','london',21,1577,'Drawbridge tower demolished; Nonsuch House begun',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[21]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1599#0','london',22,1599,'Globe playhouse built',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[22]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1603#0','london',23,1603,'James I accedes',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[23]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1603#1','london',24,1603,'Plague epidemic','Boulton: plague epidemics in 1563, 1603, 1625 and 1665 each killed between 20 and 25% of Londoners.','locations/london/events.json','$[24]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1613#0','london',25,1613,'Globe burns; rebuilt 1614','Burned 29 June 1613; rebuilt with a tiled roof by 1614.','locations/london/events.json','$[25]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1622#0','london',26,1622,'Banqueting House complete','Inigo Jones, 1619–22.','locations/london/events.json','$[26]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1625#0','london',27,1625,'Plague epidemic','Boulton: plague epidemics in 1563, 1603, 1625 and 1665 each killed between 20 and 25% of Londoners.','locations/london/events.json','$[27]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('london/1625#1','london',28,1625,'Death of James I — end of timeline',NULL,'locations/london/events.json','$[28]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-3870#0','stonehenge',0,-3870,'Coneybury Anomaly feasting pit: earliest cattle bone 3950-3790 BCE','English Heritage: the earliest cattle bone from the Stonehenge landscape, 3950-3790 BC, came from the Coneybury Anomaly, a feasting pit. That dates one bone, not every use of the pit; the tick is placed at the middle of the range.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-3600#0','stonehenge',1,-3600,'Robin Hood''s Ball and first long barrows','Approximate. Robin Hood''s Ball (causewayed enclosure, outside this map) is dated ''centuries around 3500 BC'' by English Heritage; Amesbury 42 long barrow gave 3640 to 3370 cal BCE. Most other long barrows are undated.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-3500#0','stonehenge',2,-3500,'Greater Cursus built','A red deer antler in the west terminal ditch gave 3630 to 3375 BCE; a 2009 paper found another antler about a millennium younger, so the date is debated. Whether the Cursus or the Amesbury 42 barrow is older is unknown.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-3000#0','stonehenge',3,-3000,'Stonehenge Stage 1: ditch, bank, Aubrey Holes','Darvill et al. 2012 Table 3 gives 3000 to 2620 BCE for Stage 1; the ditch itself is modelled at about 2955 to 2830 BCE (68%), and 2990 to 2755 BCE at 95%. The model switches it on in 3001 to 2995 BCE. The Aubrey Holes may predate the ditch.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2900#0','stonehenge',4,-2900,'Cremation burials at Stonehenge (c. 3000 to 2600 BCE)','More than 150 people were probably buried as cremations in the Aubrey Holes and ditch; the span is a range, not an event. One cremated woman beside Aubrey Hole 7 probably predates the ditch.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2620#0','stonehenge',5,-2620,'Stage 2 begins: trilithons, bluestone double circle','Start of Darvill et al.''s Stage 2 (2620 to 2480 BCE). Order within the stage is not settled: trilithons first, then sarsen circle, or all in quick succession.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2620#1','stonehenge',6,-2620,'Woodhenge timber rings raised (c. 2635-2575 BCE)','Historic England 2024 chronological model (95% range); the tick is a display year.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[6]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2560#0','stonehenge',7,-2560,'Sarsen circle raised (modelled c. 2560 to 2470 BCE)','The English Heritage Bayesian model puts the sarsen circle at 2580 to 2475 BCE and the trilithons at 2585 to 2400 BCE (93%). The span shown is a modelling choice inside those ranges.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[7]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2515#0','stonehenge',8,-2515,'Durrington Walls village','Eight houses excavated in 2006 about 5 m square, dated 2600 to 2500 BCE at first; later modelling gives an occupation of only about 10 to 12 years ending c. 2500 to 2460 BCE. The number of houses is unknown (a few dozen is shown, conjecturally).','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[8]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2480#0','stonehenge',9,-2480,'Durrington timber ring replaced by the henge bank','About 300 posts in a ring 440 to 450 m across were pulled and the sockets packed with chalk; the henge bank and ditch were completed c. 2480 to 2450 BCE. Also the start of Stonehenge Stage 3 (2480 to 2280 BCE).','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[9]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2400#0','stonehenge',10,-2400,'Woodhenge ditch and bank built (display date c. 2400 BCE)','Historic England''s 2024 model: timber settings 2635-2575 cal BC; ditch and bank 2495-2180 cal BC (93%), most likely 2465-2345. 2400 BCE is a display date.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[10]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2400#1','stonehenge',11,-2400,'West Amesbury henge (Bluestonehenge): stones removed','Stone sockets for up to about 27 stones, removed 2469 to 2286 BCE; the ditch and bank are c. 2400 BCE. That the stones were bluestones that went to Stonehenge is suspected, not proven.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[11]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2340#0','stonehenge',12,-2340,'Beaker burials: Amesbury Archer and a Stonehenge ditch burial','The Amesbury Archer (c. 2340 to 2300 BCE, grave outside this map) and a Beaker burial in the Stonehenge ditch (2340 to 2195 BCE) are of similar date.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[12]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2315#0','stonehenge',13,-2315,'The Avenue is built','Darvill: 2430 to 2200 BCE; English Heritage: four antler picks date construction to 2345 to 2200 BCE. About 2.5 to 2.8 km long, its first 530 m or so straight on the solstice axis. -2315 is a recommended display date inside the ranges.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[13]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-2165#0','stonehenge',14,-2165,'Bluestone circle and oval (Stage 4)','Stage 4 is 2280 to 2020 BCE: circle modelled at about 2165, oval about 2060 BCE. Around 80 bluestones were used in all; 43 survive as stones or stumps.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[14]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-1950#0','stonehenge',15,-1950,'Bush Barrow burial (c. 1950 BCE)','A rich Early Bronze Age burial on Normanton Down with a gold lozenge plate. The date of about 1950 BCE is from Wikipedia; the other grave goods are not itemised in the research notes.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[15]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-1800#0','stonehenge',16,-1800,'Z holes dug (date uncertain)','English Heritage''s model gives 2020 to 1745 BCE; its web page says 1800 to 1500 BCE. The holes were filled with windblown sediment. Purpose unknown.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[16]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-1590#0','stonehenge',17,-1590,'Y holes dug; carvings on the sarsens','Y holes 1630 to 1525 BCE (modelled). Axe and dagger carvings are dated 1750 to 1500 BCE. The last prehistoric structural change, with the stone settings ending 1630 to 1340 BCE.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[17]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-1500#0','stonehenge',18,-1500,'Middle Bronze Age fields in the east','A 2015 to 2016 survey found early fields and enclosures in the eastern part of the World Heritage Site. The sources say only ''Middle Bronze Age''; the date shown is the brief''s.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[18]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/-700#0','stonehenge',19,-700,'Vespasian''s Camp hillfort (placeholder date)','Iron Age hillfort of about 14 ha above the Avon. No construction date was retrieved; c. 700 to 600 BCE is a placeholder. The name comes from Camden and is not Roman.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[19]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/375#0','stonehenge',20,375,'Roman coins and pottery at the stones (late 4th century)','Late 4th-century coins and pottery were found at Stonehenge; Darvill thinks some bluestones were broken or removed in Roman times, his view not shared by all. A Roman building lay near the Cuckoo Stone.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[20]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/660#0','stonehenge',21,660,'Execution burial at Stonehenge (600 to 690 CE)','A decapitated man aged 28 to 32 was buried inside the stones. One source disputes the range; ''second half of the 7th century'' is the safer wording. Low confidence on the exact span.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[21]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/979#0','stonehenge',22,979,'Amesbury Abbey founded','A Benedictine house founded c. 979 by Queen Aelfthryth. Its buildings and site within Amesbury are not known from the sources read.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[22]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1086#0','stonehenge',23,1086,'Domesday Book: Amblesberie, 111 households, eight mills','Amesbury already a crossing of the London to Exeter road.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[23]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1130#0','stonehenge',24,1130,'Henry of Huntingdon names ''Stanenges'' (c. 1130)','One of England''s four wonders in his Historia Anglorum, the stones ''raised up like gates''. The date is approximate.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[24]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1136#0','stonehenge',25,1136,'Geoffrey of Monmouth: Merlin and the Giants'' Dance (c. 1136)','In the Historia Regum Britanniae Merlin moves the stones from Mount Killaraus in Ireland. A legend, not evidence of construction.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[25]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1177#0','stonehenge',26,1177,'Amesbury refounded as a Fontevraud priory','The abbey was replaced by a priory of the Order of Fontevraud. The medieval buildings are not located in the sources read.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[26]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1457#0','stonehenge',27,1457,'Hardyng: stones ''somwat bowand'' (c. 1457)','Hardyng''s Chronicle says the stones ''hang'' and are somewhat bowed: the only medieval hint of leaning.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[27]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1540#0','stonehenge',28,1540,'Amesbury Priory dissolved (1539 or 1540)','Sources conflict: the project brief says 1539, the Wikipedia Amesbury article says c. 1540. The estate passed to Edward Seymour, Earl of Hertford.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[28]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1574#0','stonehenge',29,1574,'Lucas de Heere watercolour (1573 to 75)','The first view drawn on site. The date is a range; the accompanying text was not read.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[29]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1586#0','stonehenge',30,1586,'Camden''s Britannia describes Stonehenge (1586)','Three circles within a trench, some stones 28 feet high with lintels on top. 1586 is the first edition; later editions followed.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[30]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/1620#0','stonehenge',31,1620,'Inigo Jones surveys for James I; Buckingham digs','James I visited Wilton in 1620 and commissioned a survey (per Webb''s 1655 publication); the same year the Duke of Buckingham made the first known excavation in the centre. Jones''s plan is an idealised restoration, not a record.','locations/stonehenge/events.json','$[31]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-4000#0','england-wales',0,-4000,'Farming reaches Britain','Around 4000 BCE; the date is a rounded convention.','national/events.json','$[0]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-3650#0','england-wales',1,-3650,'Long barrows and causewayed enclosures','West Kennet, Windmill Hill, Hambledon Hill and the first enclosure at Maiden Castle (about 5,500 years ago).','national/events.json','$[1]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-3000#0','england-wales',2,-3000,'Stonehenge: first ditch and bank','About 3000 BCE.','national/events.json','$[2]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-2850#0','england-wales',3,-2850,'Avebury henge begun','Built and altered from about 2850 to about 2200 BCE.','national/events.json','$[3]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-2600#0','england-wales',4,-2600,'Deep flint mining begins at Grime''s Graves','About 2600 to 2100 BCE.','national/events.json','$[4]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-2500#0','england-wales',5,-2500,'Sarsens and bluestones raised at Stonehenge','About 2500 BCE.','national/events.json','$[5]');
INSERT INTO "event"("id","location_id","seq","year","label","note","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/-2400#0','england-wales',6,-2400,'Silbury Hill built; first metal and Beaker pottery','The mound was raised between about 2470 and 2350 BCE. Copper, gold and Beaker burials appear in Britain at about the same time.','national/events.json','$[6]');
