INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-brunanburh','england-wales','battle-brunanburh','Battle of Brunanburh',53.336,-2.98,'england','battle','attested-documentary','low','Aethelstan defeated the allied kings of Dublin, Scotland and Strathclyde, securing the new kingdom of the English.','The location is unknown: over forty sites have been proposed. The marker is at Bromborough on the Wirral, the most widely supported candidate. Date (late 937) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as unlocated.',NULL,'national/places.json','$[153]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-maldon','england-wales','battle-maldon','Battle of Maldon',51.7177,0.7021,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','Ealdorman Byrhtnoth of Essex was killed fighting a Viking fleet on 10 or 11 August 991; payments of tribute followed.','The causeway to Northey Island fits the Old English poem best but is not proven. Date (10 (possibly 11) August 991) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as probably on the mainland opposite Northey Island, though other sites have been suggested. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Maldon 991'' (list entry 1000019).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[154]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-brentford-1016','england-wales','battle-brentford-1016','Battle of Brentford',51.479,-0.307,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','Edmund Ironside crossed the Thames at Brentford two days after relieving London and drove Cnut''s army off, at heavy cost to his own men.','Known mainly from the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle (1016: the king ''went over at Brentford'', fought and put the enemy to flight, many English drowning); the exact ground and the month are uncertain.','brentford','national/places.json','$[155]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-assandun','england-wales','battle-assandun','Battle of Assandun',51.606,0.692,'england','battle','attested-documentary','low','On 18 October 1016 Cnut defeated Edmund Ironside; Edmund died weeks later and Cnut took the whole kingdom.','Either Ashingdon (shown) or Ashdon, both in Essex; the question is open. Date (18 October 1016) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as unlocated.',NULL,'national/places.json','$[156]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-fulford','england-wales','battle-fulford','Battle of Fulford',53.931,-1.071,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','On 20 September 1066 Harald Hardrada and Tostig defeated the northern earls Edwin and Morcar outside York.','The exact field is disputed. Date (20 September 1066) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as approximately located around Germany Beck and Fulford Ings.',NULL,'national/places.json','$[157]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-stamford-bridge','england-wales','battle-stamford-bridge','Battle of Stamford Bridge',53.9869,-0.9034,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','On 25 September 1066 Harold Godwinson surprised and killed Harald Hardrada and Tostig.','Date (25 September 1066) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as different interpretations of where the battle was fought have been suggested. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Stamford Bridge 1066'' (list entry 1000035).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[158]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-hastings','england-wales','battle-hastings','Battle of Hastings',50.912,0.487,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','On 14 October 1066 William of Normandy defeated and killed King Harold. Battle Abbey was founded on the site about 1071.','Date (14 October 1066) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as probably accurately located in the fields south of Battle Abbey. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Hastings 1066'' (list entry 1000013).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[159]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-standard','england-wales','battle-standard','Battle of the Standard',54.3766,-1.4441,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','On 22 August 1138 a northern English army raised by Archbishop Thurstan defeated David I of Scotland near Northallerton.','Date (22 August 1138) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as probably accurately located north of Northallerton. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Northallerton 1138'' (list entry 1000027).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[160]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-lewes','england-wales','battle-lewes','Battle of Lewes',50.8818,-0.0148,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','Simon de Montfort captured Henry III and the Lord Edward on 14 May 1264 and ruled in the king''s name for a year.','The date, 14 May 1264, is secure. The Battlefields Trust places the battle on the north side of the town but says it is uncertain exactly where between the top of Offham Hill and the medieval town it was fought; the marker is a reference point, not a surveyed battle centre. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Lewes 1264'' (list entry 1000018).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[161]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-evesham','england-wales','battle-evesham','Battle of Evesham',52.106,-1.946,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','The Lord Edward trapped and killed Simon de Montfort on 4 August 1265.','Date (4 August 1265) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as approximately located on Greenhill north of Evesham. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Evesham 1265'' (list entry 1000010).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[162]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-nevilles-cross','england-wales','battle-nevilles-cross','Battle of Neville''s Cross',54.777,-1.6,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','A northern English army defeated and captured David II of Scotland outside Durham on 17 October 1346.','Date (17 October 1346) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as accurately located north of Neville''s Cross. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Neville''s Cross 1346'' (list entry 1000024).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[163]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-otterburn','england-wales','battle-otterburn','Battle of Otterburn',55.2397,-2.1919,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','A Scottish raiding army defeated the Percys in a moonlit fight in Redesdale.','The exact site is debated. Date (5 August 1388) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as approximately located north-west of Otterburn village. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Otterburn 1388'' (list entry 1000029). Chroniclers disagree on the day (5 or 19 August).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[164]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-shrewsbury','england-wales','battle-shrewsbury','Battle of Shrewsbury',52.7502,-2.7294,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','Henry IV defeated the Percy rebellion and Henry ''Hotspur'' Percy was killed, on 21 July 1403.','Date (21 July 1403) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as securely located west of St Mary Magdalene''s church at Battlefield. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Shrewsbury 1403'' (list entry 1000033).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[165]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-st-albans-1455','england-wales','battle-st-albans-1455','First Battle of St Albans',51.752,-0.339,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','A street fight on 22 May 1455 in which Richard of York captured Henry VI; usually counted as the opening of the Wars of the Roses.','Date (22 May 1455) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as securely located among the streets of the city centre.',NULL,'national/places.json','$[166]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-towton','england-wales','battle-towton','Battle of Towton',53.84,-1.268,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','Fought in snow on Palm Sunday, 29 March 1461. Edward IV''s victory secured him the throne; it is often called the largest and bloodiest battle on English soil.','Army sizes (50,000-65,000 combined) and casualty figures are chroniclers'' estimates. Date (29 March 1461) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as securely located between Saxton and Towton. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Towton, 1461'' (list entry 1000040).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[167]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-barnet','england-wales','battle-barnet','Battle of Barnet',51.666,-0.199,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','On Easter Sunday, 14 April 1471, Edward IV defeated and killed Warwick ''the Kingmaker'' in fog north of Barnet.','The battlefield lies somewhere between about 800 and 1,600 m north of Barnet; surveys have not yet fixed it. Date (14 April 1471) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as north of Barnet, but not yet precisely located. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Barnet 1471'' (list entry 1000001). The marker (Hadley Highstone) is 0.37 km north of the centroid of the registered battlefield and was left where it is, between the two candidate areas.','barnet','national/places.json','$[168]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-tewkesbury','england-wales','battle-tewkesbury','Battle of Tewkesbury',51.9819,-2.1635,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','On 4 May 1471 Edward IV destroyed the last Lancastrian army; Edward, Prince of Wales, was killed.','Date (4 May 1471) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as securely located south of Tewkesbury. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Tewkesbury 1471'' (list entry 1000039).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[169]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-bosworth','england-wales','battle-bosworth','Battle of Bosworth',52.584,-1.4186,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','On 22 August 1485 Henry Tudor defeated Richard III, the last English king to die in battle.','Survey finds published in 2009-10 moved the fighting about 3 km south-west of the traditional site on Ambion Hill. The marker now sits at the Battlefields Trust''s grid reference in the Fenn Lanes area, inside the enlarged registered battlefield; it is a reference point, not a surveyed centre. Date (22 August 1485) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Bosworth (Field) 1485'' (list entry 1000004).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[170]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-stoke-field','england-wales','battle-stoke-field','Battle of Stoke Field',53.0336,-0.8946,'england','battle','attested-documentary','medium','Henry VII defeated the Yorkist army backing Lambert Simnel on 16 June 1487, the last battle of the Wars of the Roses.','Date (16 June 1487) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as approximately located west of East Stoke. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Stoke (Field) 1487'' (list entry 1000036).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[171]');
INSERT INTO "place"("id","location_id","local_id","name","lat","lon","country","kind","evidence","confidence","summary","uncertainty","site_id","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('england-wales/battle-flodden','england-wales','battle-flodden','Battle of Flodden',55.627,-2.1671,'england','battle','attested-documentary','high','On 9 September 1513 the Earl of Surrey defeated a Scottish invasion; James IV and much of the Scottish nobility were killed.','Date (9 September 1513) confirmed by the Battlefields Trust, which describes the site as securely located in the fields south of Branxton. Historic England registers it as ''Battle of Flodden 1513'' (list entry 1000011).',NULL,'national/places.json','$[172]');
