INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/55','stonehenge','55','trilithons','sarsen','upright',217.7,7.67,1.63,0.91,6.55,'standing',0,'broken','Fallen (broken in two) in 1625, LOW confidence: Stukeley (1720s) has the south upright broken in two lying on the altar and the impost fallen on the altar "through the digging of somebody"; Aubrey (c.1666-93, quoted in Long 1876) blames the under-digging for the Duke of Buckingham in 1620 for the fall of the great stone; Barclay 1895 and Long give 1620. Aubrey wrote decades after and the exact date is not recorded, so the fall may have been after 1625; no fetched source says it was still standing in 1625.','fallen','Fallen upright of the Great Trilithon, broken in two (55a, 55b) lying across the Altar Stone; 15.8 t above ground for both pieces. Height assumed equal to 56. Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m).','{"face": 49.9, "fallToward": 49.9, "pieces": 2, "alt1625": "standing but undermined (if the fall followed the 1620 digging by years)", "dressed": true, "tenons": true}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[62]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/56','stonehenge','56','trilithons','sarsen','upright',242.1,7.67,1.63,0.91,6.55,'standing',0,'leaning','Leaning in 1625, LOW confidence (conditional on 55 having fallen): stone 56 leant over the altar against bluestone 68 in Stukeley''s time (1720s) and until it was straightened on 19 Sep 1901 (Encyclopaedia Britannica 1911: "leaning at a dangerous angle"; Barclay 1895: fall arrested by bluestone 68). It can only have leant after its partner fell, which Aubrey ties to 1620.','standing (straightened 1901)','Tallest stone at Stonehenge: 6.55 m to the platform, 6.88 m to the top of the tenon (Stones of Stonehenge). Leaning until straightened and concreted on 19 Sep 1901 (set about 0.5 m from its old position by some accounts, not verified). Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m). Lean magnitude (20 deg) is a placeholder; direction is toward the altar/centre (NE).','{"face": 49.9, "lean": 20, "leanToward": 59.9, "alt1625": "standing upright (if 55 was still standing in 1625)", "dressed": true, "tenons": true, "hTenonTop": 6.88}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[63]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/57','stonehenge','57','trilithons','sarsen','upright',266.0,7.05,2.02,1.12,5.18,'standing',0,'standing','Standing in 1625: the trilithon 57-58 with lintel 158 fell on 3 January 1797 (Maton, Archaeologia 13; Barclay 1895; Stones of Stonehenge), and Stukeley (1720s) records it entire except for part of the end of the impost. It was therefore upright in 1625 unless it fell and was re-erected, for which there is no evidence.','standing (re-erected 1958)','Fell with 58 and lintel 158 on 3 Jan 1797, re-erected 1958; height 5.18 m is an estimate (fallen when inspected in 1919). Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m).','{"face": 109.9, "dressed": true, "tenons": true}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[64]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/58','stonehenge','58','trilithons','sarsen','upright',293.4,6.46,1.87,1.04,5.18,'standing',0,'standing','Standing in 1625: the trilithon 57-58 with lintel 158 fell on 3 January 1797 (Maton, Archaeologia 13; Barclay 1895; Stones of Stonehenge), and Stukeley (1720s) records it entire except for part of the end of the impost. It was therefore upright in 1625 unless it fell and was re-erected, for which there is no evidence.','standing (re-erected 1958)','Fell with 57 and lintel 158 on 3 Jan 1797 (outwards, westwards), re-erected 1958 after drilling and pinning along a bedding plane; 5.18 m estimated. Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m).','{"face": 109.9, "dressed": true, "tenons": true}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[65]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/59','stonehenge','59','trilithons','sarsen','upright',320.6,6.2,1.81,1.01,5.03,'standing',0,'broken','Fallen and broken in 1625: Stukeley (1720s) records the other upright and the impost of this trilithon fallen forwards into the adytum and each broken in three; Barclay 1895 says the date is unknown but that a British Museum drawing (de Heere, c.1573-75) shows it was before 1574 (secondary report; the drawing was not seen). Assumed fallen in 1625.','fallen','Fallen upright, broken in three pieces (59a, 59b, 59c), fell forwards into the horseshoe; 21.8 t above ground for all three. Height assumed equal to 60. Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m).','{"face": 139.9, "fallToward": 139.9, "pieces": 3, "dressed": true, "tenons": true}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[66]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/60','stonehenge','60','trilithons','sarsen','upright',348.1,7.04,1.8,1.0,5.03,'standing',0,'standing','Standing in 1625: Stukeley (1720s) says the outer upright of the last trilithon, the jamb of the entrance to the adytum, is still standing; Barclay 1895: "Stone 60 is in situ".','standing','Only upright of the NW trilithon still standing; large natural cavity in its lower west face filled with concrete in 1959. 21.6 t. Width/thickness derived from the above-ground weight (aspect 1.8:1, taper factor 0.9), not measured; position from the horseshoe curve model (+/-1 m).','{"face": 139.9, "dressed": true, "tenons": true}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[67]');
INSERT INTO "stone"("id","location_id","local_id","set_name","type","role","az","r","w","t","h","built","hypothetical","state1625","evidence1625","state_today","notes","extra_json","origin_file","origin_path") VALUES('stonehenge/61','stonehenge','61','bluestone-horseshoe','bluestone','upright',105.7,5.68,0.73,0.46,1.75,'standing',0,'standing','Standing in 1625 (assumed as in the 1720s): Stukeley (1720s) counts only 6 stones of the inner oval "remaining upright", stumps of two on the south side by the altar, one lying behind the altar, one or two thrown down by the fall of the upright of the trilithon on the right of the entrance, and a stump of another by the upright still standing there. The stones standing today (61, 62, 63, 68, 69, 70) are exactly six. The individual identification of the fallen/stump stones (64-67, 71, 72) is my reading of Stukeley''s positions and the Stones of Stonehenge notes, not a stated mapping.','standing','Northeasternmost upstanding bluestone of the inner horseshoe; stump 61a about 2 m to one side. 0.96 t. Size from weight/height where given, else typical.','{"face": 319.9, "posQuality": "curve model, 12 stones evenly spaced 2.1 m apart along an oval 1.5 m inside the trilithons; checked against the relationships in the sources (66 under 55b, 68 against 56, 69 by 57, 71 by 59a), +/-1 m"}','locations/stonehenge/stones.json','$.stones[68]');
